Package: fastsae
Type: Package
Title: Fast Frequentist and Bayesian Small Area Estimation
Version: 1.0.0
Authors@R: c(
      person("Ridson", "Al Farizal P", role = c("aut", "cre","cph"), email = "alfrzlp@gmail.com", comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0003-0617-0214")),
      person("Azka", "Ubaidillah", role = "aut", email = "azka@stis.ac.id", comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-3597-0459")) 
    )
Description: Small area estimation (SAE) methods for survey data, covering both
    frequentist and Bayesian approaches. Frequentist models are fitted with
    compiled C++ code ('Rcpp', 'RcppArmadillo') and optional 'OpenMP'
    multi-threading. They include the area-level Fay-Herriot model (Fay and
    Herriot, 1979 <doi:10.1080/01621459.1979.10482505>), the spatial
    Fay-Herriot model (Pratesi and Salvati, 2008
    <doi:10.1007/s10260-007-0061-9>), the spatio-temporal Fay-Herriot model
    (Marhuenda et al., 2013 <doi:10.1016/j.csda.2012.09.002>), the unit-level
    Battese-Harter-Fuller model (Battese et al., 1988
    <doi:10.1080/01621459.1988.10478561>) and the two-fold subarea model
    (Torabi and Rao, 2014 <doi:10.1016/j.jmva.2014.02.001>). Bayesian models
    are fitted with Integrated Nested Laplace Approximations ('INLA'), which
    must be installed separately. The Empirical Best Predictor (EBP) is
    available for generalized linear area-level models (Gaussian, Binomial,
    Poisson, Negative Binomial, Beta, and Gamma). Further features include
    analytical and bootstrap mean squared error estimation, external and
    internal benchmarking of estimates to aggregate population targets (Rao
    and Molina, 2015 <doi:10.1002/9781118735855>), automatic handling of
    unsampled domains, and S3 methods for summaries, diagnostics and plots.
